!!id:"https://tson.io/test-suite/schemas/link-sidecar.tn"
!!meta:"https://tson.io/2026/35/m/meta.tn"
!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/35/m/core.tn"
!!import:"https://tson.io/test-suite/schemas/sidecar-common.tn"
@doc:"""
  Formal shape of a link-layer sidecar (tests/class2/link/**/*-expected.tn) -- what [TSON-SCHEMA]
  §2.2.3's import closure, §5.4's derived disjointness and §5.10.1's productivity rule make of a
  schema that has already resolved. See README.md's "The Class 2 layers" and RUNNER.md.

  Where the schema layer compares a whole resolved document, this layer states individual facts
  about the linked namespace. That is deliberate and not laziness: linking derives three things and
  merges a fourth, and a vector that exercises one of them should not have to write out every
  entry of core.tn to say so. Each list is optional, so a vector states only what it is about, and
  a sidecar stating none of them is an empty claim that `check_vectors.py` cannot catch but a
  reviewer can.
  """
{
  @doc:"""
    A choice entry and the disjointness §5.4 derives for it. The fact is total and two-valued --
    `true` exactly when every variant occupies a distinct discrimination class -- so a vector may
    state either value, and `false` is as much a claim as `true`.
    """
  disjointness => {
    name: text
    value: boolean
  }

  @doc:"A named entry and the entries §8.2's resolver-managed `subtypes` index must list under it, in any order."
  subtyping => {
    name: text
    subtypes: [text]
  }

  @doc:"""
    What the linked namespace must show. `binds` names entries the closure must resolve, which is
    how §2.2.3's "an !!import contributes the imported schema's entire namespace" is stated from
    outside an implementation: a name reachable only through an import's own import is the case
    worth writing.
    """
  link_valid => {
    binds: [text]?
    disjoint: [disjointness]?
    subtypes: [subtyping]?
  }

  @doc:"""
    An `error` vector's category is `resolver` throughout this layer, for the same [TSON-DATA] §8.1
    reason the schema layer's is: linking is part of making a schema load, and every failure to load
    or ingest one is a resolver error. A schema that will not link is never a `validation` failure,
    which is reserved for data checked against a schema that did load.
    """
  link_sidecar => sidecar_common & spliced_header & {
    ( valid: link_valid | error: vector_error )
  }
}
